### Expected Behavior

Calling `postMessage()` on a `UtilityProcess` whose child has exited is a no-op (or throws), and the main process keeps running — which is what happens everywhere *outside* the `'exit'` listener: every post returns `undefined` and is silently dropped, including posts to a SIGKILLed child that has not been reaped yet, bursts of 1000 posts, and 5 MB messages.

### Actual Behavior

Called synchronously **inside** `child.on('exit', …)` after the child was killed, `postMessage()` kills the **main process**. No exception is thrown and no event is emitted first. The macOS crash report shows:

```
EXC_BAD_ACCESS (SIGSEGV) KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS at 0x00000000000000f8
Thread: CrBrowserMain
native frame called from JavaScript under node::InternalMakeCallback
```

### Testcase Gist URL

_None — the two-file reproduction is inline below._

### Additional Information

**Reproduction.** Save both files in one directory and run `electron main.js` (default `--mode=sigkill`; also try `--mode=kill`, and `--mode=deferred` as the control).

`child.js`:

```js
// Utility process: stay alive until killed.
process.parentPort.on('message', () => {});
setInterval(() => {}, 1000);
```

`main.js`:

```js
// Minimal reproduction: postMessage() from inside a UtilityProcess 'exit'
// listener crashes the main process when the child was killed.
const { app, utilityProcess } = require('electron');
const path = require('node:path');

const mode = process.argv.find((a) => a.startsWith('--mode='))?.slice(7) ?? 'sigkill';
app.on('window-all-closed', () => {}); // no windows in this repro

app.whenReady().then(() => {
  const child = utilityProcess.fork(path.join(__dirname, 'child.js'));
  child.once('spawn', () => {
    child.once('exit', (code) => {
      console.log(`[${mode}] exit ${code}; posting from inside the exit listener`);
      child.postMessage({ hello: 'after exit' });
      console.log(`[${mode}] postMessage returned`);
    });
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (mode === 'sigkill') process.kill(child.pid, 'SIGKILL');
      else if (mode === 'kill') child.kill();
      else if (mode === 'deferred') {
        // control: same kill, but the post is deferred out of the listener
        child.removeAllListeners('exit');
        child.once('exit', () => setImmediate(() => { child.postMessage({ hello: 'deferred' }); console.log('[deferred] postMessage returned'); }));
        process.kill(child.pid, 'SIGKILL');
      }
    }, 300);
  });
  setTimeout(() => { console.log(`[${mode}] main process still alive`); app.exit(0); }, 2500);
});
```

When the post in the `'exit'` listener crashes main, neither "postMessage returned" nor "main process still alive" is printed.

**Results** (each run in a fresh Electron process; counts combine a larger measurement harness and the minimal repro above):

| How the child ended | Post made | Main process crashed |
|---|---|---|
| SIGKILL from outside | synchronously inside the `'exit'` listener | 16 / 16 (minimal repro: 2 / 3) |
| `process.abort()` in the child | synchronously inside the `'exit'` listener | 16 / 16 |
| `child.kill()` | synchronously inside the `'exit'` listener | 15 / 17 (minimal repro: 3 / 3) |
| `process.exit()` in the child | synchronously inside the `'exit'` listener | 0 / 16 |
| SIGKILL or `child.kill()` | in a microtask, `setImmediate` or `setTimeout 0` queued by the `'exit'` listener | 0 (10 / 10 survived each; minimal repro deferred control: 3 / 3 survived) |
| SIGKILL or `child.kill()` | `'exit'` listener that posts nothing (control) | 0 / 20 |

**Workaround.** Latch an "exited" flag in the first `'exit'` listener (registered right after `fork`) and never call `postMessage()` once it is set.

