Passing an extension background page's `webContents` into `new WebContentsView({ webContents })` segfaults the main process.

```js
const { app, session, WebContentsView } = require('electron')

app.whenReady().then(async () => {
  const bgPage = new Promise((resolve) => {
    app.on('web-contents-created', (_e, wc) => {
      if (wc.getType() === 'backgroundPage') resolve(wc)
    })
  })
  // any MV2 extension with a background page, e.g. spec/fixtures/extensions/persistent-background-page
  await session.defaultSession.extensions.loadExtension(extensionPath)
  new WebContentsView({ webContents: await bgPage })
})
```

```
Received signal 11 SEGV_MAPERR 000000000078
electron::WebContentsPreferences::Clear()                 web_contents_preferences.cc:199
electron::WebContentsPreferences::SetFromDictionary()     web_contents_preferences.cc:243
electron::api::WebContents::CreateFromWebPreferences()    electron_api_web_contents.cc:5291
electron::api::WebContentsView::New()                     electron_api_web_contents_view.cc:230
```

Background pages are created without a `WebContentsPreferences`, and when `CreateFromWebPreferences` adopts an existing `webContents` it uses `WebContentsPreferences::From()` without a null check. The owner window check in `WebContentsView::New` doesn't catch it because a background page has no owner window (DevTools contents, for example, get rejected there with "already attached to a window" and are fine).

Reproduced on main on Linux. The same unguarded code is on 42-x-y through main, and the `webContents` option has been there since #42086 (v32).

Probably simplest to throw here like we do for the owner window case, I don't think hosting a background page in a view is something we mean to support?
